  en: {
    series: `Avatar Tips #${article.no}`,
    title: "Gimmicks that react to touch",
    lead: "Your cheeks turn red when someone pats your head. Your face looks surprised when someone grabs your tail. VRChat's Contacts and PhysBone parameters tell the Animator that you were touched. This tip explains how they work, how to build them yourself, and how they look to other people.",
    meta: "This covers Unity's Built-in Render Pipeline and VRChat avatars (Avatars 3.0). Accurate as of November 2026.",
    publishedLabel: "Published",
    tocLabel: "Contents",
    whenTitle: "Read this when",
    when: [
      "You want your avatar to react with an expression or a blush when someone pats your head.",
      "You want your expression to change when someone grabs your hair or tail.",
      "Nothing happens when someone touches you. Or it reacts to your own hands too.",
      "You can see the reaction yourself, but you don't know whether other people can see it.",
    ],
    whatTitle: "How Contacts work",
    whatLead: `A Contact is a VRChat component for detecting a "touch". It's separate from Unity's normal collision (Colliders). It puts whether something is touching into a parameter, like the ones you saw in ${tipLink("animator-basics", "parameters")}.`,
    what: [
      { head: "VRC Contact Sender (the sending side)", body: "A component that only says \"I'm here\". VRChat automatically adds Senders to the head, body, hands, feet and fingers of humanoid avatars." },
      { head: "VRC Contact Receiver (the receiving side)", body: "A component that puts a value into an Animator parameter when a Sender enters it. You place it yourself where you want a reaction." },
      { head: "Collision Tags", body: "It only reacts when the Sender and the Receiver share at least one tag. Tags are case-sensitive." },
      { head: "Allow Self and Allow Others", body: "These decide whether the Receiver reacts to Senders on your own avatar (Allow Self), to other people's Senders (Allow Others), or both." },
      { head: "Local Only", body: "It only works in your own VRChat. It doesn't work on other people's screens. In return, it doesn't count toward your Performance Rank." },
    ],
    what2: [
      `The Senders VRChat adds automatically have the tags listed on ${ext(links.tags, "the Built-In Contact Tags page")}. The ones you'll use most are <code>Head</code>, <code>Torso</code> (body), <code>Hand</code> (either hand), <code>HandL</code> and <code>HandR</code> (left and right hand), <code>Finger</code> (any finger) and <code>Foot</code>. Put these tags on a Receiver, and it also reacts to the hands and fingers of other people's avatars.`,
      "You can adjust the position and size of the automatic Senders in the <code>Colliders</code> section of the VRC Avatar Descriptor. These Senders don't count toward your Performance Rank.",
    ],
    typesTitle: "Choosing a Receiver Type",
    typesLead: "What the Receiver puts into the Animator parameter is chosen with <code>Receiver Type</code>.",
    typesTableLabel: "Receiver Types, the values they give, and what they're good for",
    typesHead: ["Receiver Type", "Parameter type", "Value", "Good for"],
    typesRows: [
      ["Constant", "Bool (Float or Int also work)", "true (1) the whole time something is touching. false (0) when it leaves", "Changing your expression or blushing only while touched"],
      ["OnEnter", "Bool (Float or Int also work)", "true (1) for only one frame at the moment of touch. Goes back on the next frame", "Starting something once when touched (playing a sound, playing an animation once)"],
      ["Proximity", "Float only", "0 to 1. Gets closer to 1 nearer the center of the Receiver", "Changing something more the closer it gets (how strong a blush is, how much the eyes narrow)"],
    ],
    types2: [
      "The Proximity value is how close the surface of the Sender is to the center of the Receiver. It goes just above 0 when the Sender touches the Receiver's edge, and reaches 1 when the Sender reaches the center. When several Senders are inside, you get the value of the closest one.",
      "OnEnter lets you set <code>Min Velocity</code>. With it, the Receiver only reacts when something enters faster than a certain speed. You can use it for things like \"don't react to a light touch, only to a pat\".",
    ],
    demoTitle: "Try it out",
    demoLead: "Imagine a sphere Receiver with a 15 cm radius above your head (Collision Tags are Hand and Finger). Change how far the hand is, whose hand it is, and Allow Self and Allow Others, and look at the value for each Receiver Type and the blush result.",
    demo: {
      side: "Side view",
      faceLabel: "Face",
      distance: "Distance from the hand to the Receiver's center (cm)",
      who: "Hand doing the patting",
      whos: [
        { value: "others", text: "Someone else's hand" },
        { value: "self", text: "Your own hand" },
      ],
      filter: "Receiver Filtering",
      type: "Receiver Type connected to the blush",
      types: [
        { value: "constant", text: "Constant (Bool. 100 while touching)" },
        { value: "onenter", text: "OnEnter (Bool)" },
        { value: "proximity", text: "Proximity (Float. 0 to 100 with Motion Time)" },
      ],
      valuesLabel: "Values the Receiver puts in",
      enter: "1 (only for one frame as the hand enters)",
      whyOut: "The hand is outside the Receiver's sphere (radius {r} cm). Every Receiver Type gives 0.",
      whyIn: "The hand is inside the sphere. Proximity gets closer to 1 nearer the center, and is {p} now.",
      whySelf: "The hand is inside the sphere, but it's your own hand and Allow Self is off, so the Receiver doesn't react.",
      whyOthers: "The hand is inside the sphere, but it's someone else's hand and Allow Others is off, so the Receiver doesn't react.",
      resultOn: "Blush: {b}. Your cheeks turn red.",
      resultOff: "Blush: 0. Your cheeks don't turn red.",
