        ],
      }],
      notes: [
        "Make the sphere big enough to cover the top of your head.",
        "Reacts to other people's hands. To react to your own hands too, check Allow Self as well.",
        "These are the tags on the Senders VRChat automatically adds to hands and fingers.",
        "Puts a Float that gets closer to 1 the nearer something is into a parameter called HeadPat.",
      ],
    },
    clipLabel: "2. Make the blush clip",
    clip: [
      `Right-click in the Project window and choose <code>Create → Animation</code> to make a clip called <code>Blush</code>. See ${tipLink("animation-clip", "record")} for how to record.`,
      "In the Animation window, switch the clip to <code>Blush</code> and press the record button. Move the time bar to 0 and set the face mesh's blush BlendShape (e.g. <code>cheek_blush</code>) to 0.",
      "Move the time bar to 1:00 (1 second) and set the blush BlendShape to 100. Stop recording. With Motion Time, the start of the clip is 0 and the end is 1, so if HeadPat is 0.5, the blush is 50.",
      "Don't write anything other than the blush BlendShape.",
    ],
    layerLabel: "3. Make a layer in FX",
    layer: [
      "Open your Animator Controller in the Animator window, choose <code>Float</code> from <code>+</code> in the <code>Parameters</code> tab, and name it <code>HeadPat</code>. Match the name you typed in the Receiver exactly, including upper and lower case.",
      "Don't add <code>HeadPat</code> to Expression Parameters. The Receiver sets the value, so you don't need to change it from the menu.",
